WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.808
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.015
SIMON WARDLEY: Hello.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:10.900
Hello.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.090
Hello.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.470
Yay.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.490
It's the final session
of the conference.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:20.500
Well done for getting this far.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.850
My name is Simon Wardley.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.320
I'm going to talk to you about
crossing the river by feeling

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.480
the stones.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.000
Couple of warnings
before I start.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.220
I'm going to go through an awful
lot and we've only got an hour.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.760
So I've done a quick graph.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.200
Level of audience pain,
that's you, against the number

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.070
of slides given in an
hour's presentation.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.843
There's a safe limit
of around about 40.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:57.340
Since I like to experiment,
I'll be using 362.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.410
So this is going to go
really, really quickly.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.220
So save that-- run away, OK.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.580
The other thing is, I'm
going to talk about strategy.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.330
I don't like the subject much.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.240
I normally expect these
people to turn up and tell me,

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:18.130
agile all the things,
or whatever's popular.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.460
These people-- I
mean consultants.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:27.250
Things like IoT everything,
AI everything, et cetera.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.880
Or my favorite is
earlobes everything.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.470
This is from the
"Harvard Business

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.680
Review," November 2011.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.260
"How Earlobes Can Signify
Leadership Potential."

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.740
It's phrenology of
management, so it's

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.670
really important for executives
to have the right size

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:46.960
earlobes.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.090
So I'm going to
talk about strategy

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:53.080
from my own personal perspective
when I was CEO of a software

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.680
company called Fotango.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.570
We'd been bought out by Canon.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.850
We built a number of different
services, online photo

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.570
services, several
million users, et cetera.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.880
And we were doing
fine, revenue all good,

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.870
and we were profitable.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.830
But we had a problem
and the problem was me.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.370
Because rather than being
a chessmaster of strategy

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:20.020
like CEO, I was making
it up as I went along.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.330
I was a fake CEO.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.270
It was all-- well, I
had all those things

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.360
like vision statements.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.150
"Our strategy is
customer focused.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.570
We will lead an innovative
effort of the market

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.590
through our use of agile
techniques and open source."

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:38.740
And that sounded good,
except for I'd pinched it

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.320
from someone else
because I really

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.010
had no clue what I was doing.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:48.220
But what I did do is I
used to go around listening

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.130
to other CEOs talk
about strategy

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:53.170
and I started to
record the words

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.080
they said, looking for
what I call Business Level

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.582
Abstractions of a Healthy
Strategy, or BLAHS, for short.

00:03:01.582 --> 00:03:03.040
And I do this every
couple of years

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.650
and this is my recent
set of common BLAHS

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.000
that people talk about.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.710
Things like digital
business, big data,

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.230
disruptive, innovative,
collaborative, competitive,

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:15.070
advantage, ecosystem, open
source, et cetera, et cetera.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.320
And then, what I normally
do is get all these strategy

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.450
documents, pile them together,
and generate a Blah template.

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:23.544
"Our strategy is Blah.

00:03:23.544 --> 00:03:25.210
We will lead a Blah
effort of the market

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:28.240
through use of Blah and
Blah to build a Blah."

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.370
And then I combine the
Blahs and the Blah template

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.880
and also generate a whole
bunch of different strategies

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.260
at random.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:36.940
So I thought I'd
go through some.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.450
Strategy number one,
total random gibberish.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.220
"Our strategy is
customer focused.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.470
We will lead a disruptive
effort of the market

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:47.800
through our use of innovative
social media and big data

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.330
to build a collaborative
cloud based ecosystem."

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.970
Strategy two, again,
total gibberish.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:57.790
"Our strategy is innovative
digital business.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:59.740
We will lead a growth
effort of the market

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:03.220
through our use of customer
focused competitive advantage."

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.720
You get the thing.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.070
And then I send them
around to people

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:10.300
and I normally get responses
of three basic types.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.040
"This is more or less
the exact wording

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.597
from our business plan."

00:04:14.597 --> 00:04:16.180
"I've seen two of
these used already."

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.420
And my favorite is,
"Are you for hire?"

00:04:22.132 --> 00:04:24.340
By the way, a friend of mine
has put this all online.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.930
This is strategy as a service.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:33.220
So if you ever need a
strategy, just type in the URL.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.470
This is the one I did earlier.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.929
Well, I did it yesterday.

00:04:35.929 --> 00:04:36.970
You just type In the URL.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.950
If you don't like it,
just press refresh.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.340
"Our strategy is collaborative.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.048
We will lead an open
effort of the market

00:04:44.048 --> 00:04:46.120
through our use of big
data and social media."

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.960
Anyway, so there
I was in 2005, CEO

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:54.410
of this company, not knowing
a clue of what I was doing.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.350
I was like, oh, oh, dear.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:57.920
What shall I do?

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.220
So I went back to basics.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:01.060
Sun Tzu.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.540
Anybody know what Sun Tzu wrote?

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.470
"Art of War," fantastic.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.730
Sun Tzu talked
about five factors

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.170
that mattered in competition.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.950
One, understand your purpose,
your moral imperative.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.200
Two, understand the landscape
that you're competing in.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.760
Three, the climactic patterns
that impact that landscape.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.500
Four, your doctrine,
and then you're

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.880
into leadership, which is
context-specific gameplay.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.680
Then I came across John Boyd.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.302
Anybody know what
John Boyd wrote?

00:05:30.302 --> 00:05:30.927
AUDIENCE: OODA.

00:05:30.927 --> 00:05:31.760
SIMON WARDLEY: OODA.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.280
OODA loops.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.580
A US Air Force pilot.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.520
John talked about,
you have the game.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.110
The first thing you do is
observe the environment,

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.830
so that's the landscape
and the climatic patterns.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.560
You orientate around the game.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.480
And then you decide
what you're going to do.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.550
That's the
context-specific play.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:51.260
And then you act.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:53.240
Now often, people will
say, well strategy's

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.250
all about the importance of why.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.200
Well, that's fair enough,
but there are two whys.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.890
There's the why of
purpose, as in, I

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.500
want to be the best
online photo service.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.470
And the why of movement,
as in, why do I

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.240
make this move over that move?

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.490
So if you think about
a game of chess,

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.700
you've got the why of purpose,
which is to win the game,

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:15.680
and then you've got
why of movement.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.610
Should I move that piece or
should I move that piece?

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:22.220
And it's through movement
that we do learning.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.980
So we move that piece, it gives
us a tiny bit of advantage,

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.300
not much of a great deal.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.030
But if we move that
piece, checkmate, you see?

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:31.750
So much better.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:35.670
So if I go back to
Fotango, I had my purpose,

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:36.930
online photo service.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.050
Here we are, 2005,
very profitable.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.660
The next bit is, how do I
understand the landscape?

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.840
How do I observe
the environment?

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.260
This led me into a topic known
as situational awareness.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.920
And to explain this, I'm
going to use three examples.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.810
Vikings, chess,
and Themistocles.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:01.210
Vikings, a very fearsome race.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:06.580
The way Vikings did navigation
is they used stories.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.820
Things like, "From Hernam,
head due west towards Hvarf,

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.890
and you will have sailed
north of Hjaltland,

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.170
so that you just glimpse
it in clear weather."

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:17.050
That, actually, is just that.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:19.810
A much simpler translation.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:21.820
But the question
I have to you is,

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.650
what would you use to navigate?

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.170
A visual map or a verbal story?

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:26.920
What do you think?

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:31.730
Map?

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:32.264
Story?

00:07:32.264 --> 00:07:32.930
AUDIENCE: Story.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:33.680
SIMON WARDLEY: Story.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:34.230
Map.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:34.790
A map.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:35.290
OK.

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.180
What do we use in business?

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:37.930
AUDIENCE: Stories.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:38.320
SIMON WARDLEY: Stories.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:38.800
Super.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:39.300
Right.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:39.910
Excellent.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:41.720
The next example is chess world.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.750
I want you to imagine
you live in a world

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:49.470
where everybody plays chess, but
no one's ever seen the board.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.360
All you've ever seen are
these characters on a screen.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:54.130
And you play the
game very simply.

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:58.060
You press a button,
your opponent counters,

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.620
you press a button,
they counter.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:03.910
And the game goes on for
ages until somebody wins

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:05.800
or it's a draw.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.390
Now, what will
happen is people will

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:11.260
take these sequences of many
games and feed them into their,

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:14.290
I don't know, big data
systems and come up

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.880
with magic secrets of success.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.300
So if you press queen,
I should respond

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.250
with king, pawn, or whatever.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:26.800
And we'll write articles about
that, a bit like earlobes.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.380
Then what will happen is you
will play a game of chess

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:32.559
against somebody who will
see something truly magical,

00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:34.539
the board, but you
won't know this.

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:38.020
And so you'll press a
piece and they'll counter.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.570
And you'll press a piece
and they'll counter

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:42.139
and you'll have lost.

00:08:42.139 --> 00:08:43.900
And your first--
you'll be there,

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:47.631
what the fiddlesticks
happened there?

00:08:47.631 --> 00:08:48.130
All right.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:49.546
The first thing
you're going to do

00:08:49.546 --> 00:08:52.120
is scribble down that
sequence as though it's

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.550
some sort of magic
sequence which you're

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:56.680
going to try and use yourself.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:57.980
And you'll still lose.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:08:59.980
And then you'll start
thinking, well, maybe it's

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.460
the speed at which they
pressed the button.

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:05.470
Then you'll start
going, is it cultural?

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.020
They're a happy sort of person.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:11.350
Maybe that's why they beat me.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.450
They beat you because
you exist in a low level

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:14.890
situational awareness
environment.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:16.750
So what would you use
to learn, by the way?

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:18.520
Secrets of success?

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.380
Or context specific game play?

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:22.222
What do you think?

00:09:22.222 --> 00:09:23.920
[INAUDIBLE]

00:09:24.639 --> 00:09:25.680
SIMON WARDLEY: Excellent.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.420
What do you think
we use in business?

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:28.734
AUDIENCE: Secrets of success.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:30.150
SIMON WARDLEY:
Secrets of success.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:30.700
Excellent.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:31.200
Super.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:32.070
Right.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.840
The next example
is Themistocles,

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.097
ancient politician,
Greek general.

00:09:37.097 --> 00:09:37.680
Had a problem.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:39.450
The Persians were invading.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:40.200
Had choices.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.070
Defend around Athens,
defend around Thebes.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.570
What he decided to
do was to block off

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:48.420
the Straits of Artemisium,
force the Persians

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.690
along the coastal road into
Thermopylae, narrow pass,

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.750
small number of troops defend
against a larger force.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:10:02.200
So about 170,000 Persians, 4,000
Greeks, including 300 Spartans.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:03.730
Fabulous.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:07.540
So I want you to imagine you're
a member of the Athenian army,

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.430
so part of the Greek states.

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:10.690
It's the eve of battle.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.770
Themistocles is in front of you.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.670
He's giving you purpose,
a moral imperative.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.890
We must defeat the Persians.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:21.262
And then he says to you, I
don't understand the landscape.

00:10:21.262 --> 00:10:22.720
I don't understand
the environment.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:24.040
I have no map.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:29.886
But have no fear, for I
have created a SWOP diagram.

00:10:29.886 --> 00:10:32.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:33.560
Strengths.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.910
A well trained Spartan army,
a high level of motivation

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:37.860
not to become a Persian slave.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:38.490
Weaknesses.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:41.280
The Ephors might stop
the Spartans turning up.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.900
Sorry, a truckload of
Persians are turning up.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:44.489
Opportunities.

00:10:44.489 --> 00:10:46.530
Get rid of the Persians,
get rid of the Spartans.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.300
We're Athenian, we
actually hate the Spartans.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:49.800
Threats.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.600
The Persians get rid
of us and the Oracle

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.720
says a really dodgy film might
be produced a few thousand

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.651
years later.

00:10:56.651 --> 00:10:57.150
OK.

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.460
So what would you use to
communicate and determine

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.350
strategy in battle?

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:04.650
Position and movement
described by a map or some sort

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:05.820
of magic framework?

00:11:09.867 --> 00:11:12.694
What would you use in business?

00:11:12.694 --> 00:11:13.827
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:13.827 --> 00:11:15.660
SIMON WARDLEY: Excellent.
[? CPAV, ?] right.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:16.701
You're getting the point.

00:11:16.701 --> 00:11:19.600
So here I've got chess,
here I've got alchemy.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:22.000
Navigation, learning,
and strategy.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.070
In chess, it's all
visual, contextspecific.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.290
It's all about
position and movement.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:28.810
It's what we call a high
level situational awareness

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.700
environment, a bit
like the military.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.670
If you ask a general, why
did you bomb the hill?

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:36.430
They won't say, because I read
an article in "General Weekly"

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:39.970
that 67% of generals
bombard hills.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:43.620
They won't tell you, because
it would make a good story.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.270
Or that's what Uber would do.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.090
But over here, this is alchemy.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.520
This is all about
storytelling, secrets

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:53.800
of success, magic frameworks.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.950
It's all low level situational
awareness environment.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:12:00.580
And so that's where I
was as the fake CEO,

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:02.350
desperately hoping
that no one would

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:07.550
rumble that I didn't know what
on earth I was talking about.

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:10.510
Anyway, I explained this
to a couple of my peers

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:14.770
and they said, oh, don't worry,
it's all about execution.

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:17.680
Well that's interesting because
that's one of those memes,

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.510
"I'd rather have a
first-rate execution

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.820
and second-rate strategy."

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:24.140
I tend to like data, though.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.540
So I went around looking--

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:27.880
I to 160 Silicon
Valley companies,

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:30.610
looked at their level
of strategic play,

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:35.050
situational awareness from low
to high, and the use of open--

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:36.760
as in open source,
open data, open

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:39.760
APIs-- as ways of
manipulating the market.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.880
The bigger the bubbles, the
more companies in that group.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:47.020
Now, if execution ruled,
these would be doing well.

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:50.200
If strategy ruled, those
would be doing well.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:52.090
And that's what it
actually looks like.

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:54.880
Market cap changes over
a seven-year period,

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:56.980
positive, more negative.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:13:00.640
It turns out both are important.

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.030
Strategy actually matters.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:07.390
So Dimon's doctrine, as
Professor Martin said,

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:09.520
is about "as flawed
as it is popular,"

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:11.384
which is a bit of a shame.

00:13:11.384 --> 00:13:13.550
So I would explain that to
people and they would go,

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:17.780
but we're successful, getting--
it was my fat cat getting

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:21.020
a little bit angry here.

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:24.140
Which is fair enough, if
you're fighting against others.

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:26.420
It's OK to be
hopeless at this stuff

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:28.940
as long as everybody else
is because then no one gains

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:30.320
an advantage.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:33.230
And there's this
wonderful study by Fitza,

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:36.680
"Use of Variance Decomposition
in the Investigation of CEO

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:38.720
Effects," which basically
says those are no

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.770
different from random chance.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.460
So you can just get
someone off the street.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:47.690
At which point, people
get really angry at me.

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:49.220
We're not stupid!

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:50.430
Well, that's fair enough.

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:51.930
But there is a difference.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:55.170
You don't have to
be daft to be blind.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:59.640
So the Vikings themselves, they
didn't see the environment.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:03.860
They had stories, it was
their way of communication.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.540
They didn't use maps at all.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:08.110
Doesn't mean they were daft.

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:11.320
And the difference
turns out to be maps.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.780
But what do we mean by maps?

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:16.450
Well, maps have
certain characteristics

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:18.190
that are really important.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:20.950
They are visual,
context-specific, battle

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:22.000
at hand.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.100
You have the position of
pieces relative to some anchor.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:28.060
In this case, a compass.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:30.390
And you can see
consistent movement

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:33.590
and, obviously, you
have components.

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:35.680
It's the same with
the chessboard.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:37.780
Visual, context-specific.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:39.550
You have position
of pieces relative

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:42.370
to an anchor, the anchor
being the board itself,

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:43.900
and you have movement.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:47.350
And that's what a map has.

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:49.300
So what did I have in business?

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:53.380
Well, I had systems
diagrams, network topologies,

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:55.030
which are not maps.

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:58.600
They don't have those
navigation characteristics.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:14:59.710
Or I had things like this.

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:04.120
Business process maps, which
turn out not to be maps either.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.490
In fact, I had loads
of diagrams which

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.470
call themselves
maps and didn't have

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:11.990
any of the
characteristics of a map.

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:14.300
So that was my problem, 2005.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:18.110
How do I create a map?

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:20.950
So I took one of my
network topology diagrams.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:22.995
I said, right, I need an anchor.

00:15:22.995 --> 00:15:24.620
We're going to have
to start somewhere.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:27.080
So I took the customer
and the user need.

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.180
That was my anchor
at the very top.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:30.290
Great.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:32.950
Now we need position.

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:35.740
Well, I had lots of components,
which were basically

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:37.480
in a chain of needs.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:40.270
So things like, the customer
wanted print, print needed

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:43.300
a website, website needed
a platform, platform needed

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.940
compute, compute needed power.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:48.040
So I could create
a chain of needs.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:51.980
Now I've got position
and an anchor.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:53.000
Good.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.250
But I still have no movement.

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:57.280
Well, that's a problem.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.080
Because if you look at
somebody like Nokia,

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.570
it was a paper mill,
plastics manufacturer,

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:04.570
telecommunications company,
something else today.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:06.040
It's changing.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.030
So I need to describe movement.

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:10.720
So I took power.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:11.820
I thought, well, power.

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:15.090
We start off with a
Parthian Battery, 480 AD.

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:21.630
Somehow, we end up with utility
provision, 1886, Westinghouse,

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:23.100
Tesla.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.220
So how do we go
from there to there?

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:31.140
My first idea was diffusion
curves, Everett Rogers's work,

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:32.700
made famous by Geoffrey Moore.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.910
Have you heard of
"Crossing the Chasm"?

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:36.810
Yes?

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:38.670
So adoption over time.

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.460
Geoffrey Moore does it as
the non-cumulative form.

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:45.430
Everett Rogers, the original
work, is the s-curve shape.

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.660
Geoffrey, very valuably,
added this chasm,

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:52.140
the thing you have to jump
over, from the early market

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:53.940
to the mainstream market.

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:56.407
I thought, well, this is
going to be simple, isn't it?

00:16:56.407 --> 00:16:57.865
Because what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:16:57.865 --> 00:16:59.940
probably going to
get something up here

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:01.560
and we'll get custom
built examples,

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:03.910
and then probably
products and commodity.

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:06.119
And I just have to find
the right percentages

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:07.480
and, oh, that's wonderful.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:09.450
That'll be easy.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:13.380
Except for--
unfortunately, I like data.

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:15.049
So I looked at things like--

00:17:15.049 --> 00:17:17.880
well, take this, the smartphone.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:18.390
Right.

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:21.372
Who thinks the smartphone
is a commodity?

00:17:21.372 --> 00:17:24.550
Hands up.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.450
There's quite a few
of you who don't.

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:27.339
OK.

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:30.350
And what percentage
of people in the US

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:31.600
do you think have smartphones?

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.260
65%, 70%, 80%, 90%?

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:38.220
OK, fine.

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:38.960
Right.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:40.070
Gold bars.

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:42.240
Who thinks gold bars
are a commodity?

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:46.770
Is there somebody
here who doesn't think

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.080
a gold bar is a commodity?

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:49.070
OK, fine.

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:51.365
What percentage of the
population have gold bars?

00:17:53.711 --> 00:17:54.210
All right.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:56.220
The problem is, a
whole bunch of stuff

00:17:56.220 --> 00:18:00.510
wasn't commodity when 70%,
80% of the population had it.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:03.960
But a whole bunch of things
were commodity when only 1%

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:06.210
of the population had it.

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:10.532
So I can't really use adoption
as a measure of change.

00:18:10.532 --> 00:18:12.240
Turned out that things
appeared and would

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.180
evolve through multiple
diffusion curves

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:18.460
over different time frames and
different applicable markets.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:22.880
So I can actually use adoption
or time to measure change.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:26.520
So I was a bit
stymied at that point.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.410
And then I noticed something.

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:31.140
When looking back through
the history of telephones--

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:35.180
this is diffusion of multiple
improving forms of telephones.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.440
In the earlier
stages, I noticedS

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.290
we wrote manuals like this.

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:43.200
"The Telephone and
How to Use It."

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:47.210
How to hold it, how to
speak into it, et cetera.

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:50.430
And I thought, well, we
don't do that anymore.

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:53.070
And then I discovered
things like this.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:57.330
"This room is equipped
with Edison Electric Light.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:00.960
Do not attempt to
light with a match.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:04.630
Simply turn key on
wall by the door."

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:06.980
And so I started to
look into publications.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:10.110
And what happens is
something appears

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:13.290
and we have publications
talking about the wonder of it.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:15.150
The wonder of radio.

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:17.850
Then that gets overtaken
by publications talking

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:20.340
about how to build something.

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.580
Building construction awareness,
how to build your own crystal

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:24.870
radio set.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.570
Then that gets overtaken
by publications,

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:29.910
which talk about operation,
maintenance, and feature

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:31.140
differentiation.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:33.270
And finally, you
get publications

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.290
which talk about use.

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:40.090
So things like the wonder of
radio, crystal radio sets,

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:42.660
how to build one, my
radio is better or worse

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:43.890
than your radio.

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:46.740
Followed by, here's
the radio times,

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:51.220
what's good on channel
whatever at 4 o'clock?

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:55.270
And so there was increasing
certainty about the thing.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:58.930
So what I did was I took this
point, where the publication

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.720
types changed, went back
and looked and found

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:04.810
the applicable market,
then measured ubiquity

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:07.210
against the applicable
market against certainty,

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:11.680
that point of stability,
and discovered this pattern.

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:15.400
What happens is things
appear novel and new,

00:20:15.400 --> 00:20:17.650
the genesis of new
acts like the first

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:21.720
ever telephone, the first
computer, the Z3, 1943,

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.560
the Parthian battery.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:26.470
Then, through demand
and supply competition,

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:29.530
it evolves and you get
custom built examples.

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:30.640
Then you get products.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:34.930
So computers, you got
things like the IBM 650.

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:37.420
Improving products,
rental services,

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:40.480
time-share, commodity
hardware, and eventually,

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:43.090
utility services.

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:46.560
And that is movement.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:48.620
So I was able to
take my value chain,

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:52.590
flatten that evolution curve
at the bottom, add in movement,

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:56.376
and suddenly, I had a map.

00:20:56.376 --> 00:20:58.600
Quick recap.

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:00.645
I started off as this
fake CEO, no clue

00:21:00.645 --> 00:21:02.020
what I was doing
because I hadn't

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:03.640
gone to the right courses.

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:05.800
No one had taught me
how to do this stuff

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:08.650
so I had to invent my own way.

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:10.740
I realized I was
competing against others.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:13.270
I was completely blind,
totally depending

00:21:13.270 --> 00:21:17.620
upon stories, secrets of
success, and magic frameworks.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.510
So I thought, well,
hang on a minute.

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:21.820
I want to be over here.

00:21:21.820 --> 00:21:25.960
That means I need to understand,
A, that strategy's a cycle.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:27.610
That I need to
observe the landscape,

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:30.430
learn about climatic
patterns, orientate around it,

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:33.340
and then get into
context-specific play.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:36.130
Which means I need
to first understand

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:37.800
the landscape with a map.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:41.140
A map needs an anchor, so
that was users and user needs.

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:43.210
I need position
relative to an anchor,

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:44.830
so that's my value chain.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:46.030
I add in evolution.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.350
I now have a map.

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:49.017
It is 2005.

00:21:49.017 --> 00:21:51.100
And I went to other people
and said, look at this,

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:52.510
isn't it exciting?

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:55.820
And they went, so what?

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:59.450
Well, now I have a map.

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:04.070
I can start to observe
climatic impacts.

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:07.250
These are the roles
that influence the game.

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:10.070
The first one I noticed was
that everything evolves.

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:11.990
Due to supply and
demand competition,

00:22:11.990 --> 00:22:14.540
it's all moving
from left to right.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:17.720
The next one is the
characteristics change

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:18.860
as it evolves.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:21.140
It starts off in this
uncharted space, where

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:23.870
it's chaotic, uncertain,
poorly understood, changing,

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:26.960
different, exciting, and
a source of future worth.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.230
And over an unknown
amount of time,

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:33.140
it becomes industrialized,
ordered, standard, stable,

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:34.820
measured, dull, boring.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:37.250
This is known as the Salaman
and Storey Innovation

00:22:37.250 --> 00:22:41.660
Paradox of 2002.

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:46.400
So the next thing I noticed was
that, because characteristics

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:50.070
change, there's no such thing
as a one size fits all method.

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:52.850
Agile in-house was very
strong on the left,

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:55.700
very weak on the right compared
to things like Six Sigma, which

00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:58.880
was all about reducing
deviation, and very weak

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:01.670
in the middle compared to
things like Lean Enterprise

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:04.700
because they're all focused on
learning and reducing waste.

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:08.580
So there's no such thing
as one size fits all.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.830
Which was great,
because in 2002--

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:15.530
sorry, 2001, we'd gone
all Agile, as in XP.

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:19.100
And then by about 2003,
2004, we were discovering

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:20.730
it wasn't working everywhere.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:21.650
So now, we--

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:25.300
2005, we understood why.

00:23:25.300 --> 00:23:29.260
We also realized that our
purchasing methods were not

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:29.980
singular.

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:33.010
We needed time, material, VC
based here, outcome based here,

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:36.510
COTS and fixed, and utility.

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:37.610
That's good.

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:39.230
Then we learned another pattern.

00:23:39.230 --> 00:23:41.450
As things evolved, they
became more efficient,

00:23:41.450 --> 00:23:42.470
like electricity.

00:23:42.470 --> 00:23:45.590
But that enables higher
order systems to appear.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:49.252
Electricity enabling
computing, radio, television.

00:23:49.252 --> 00:23:50.960
So if you think about
the nuts and bolts,

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:53.730
used to be a home
made cottage industry.

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:56.030
Then Maudslay introduced
the screw cutting lathe.

00:23:56.030 --> 00:23:58.520
Suddenly, you get an explosion
in standard nuts and bolts

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:02.350
and suddenly, an
explosion in machinery.

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:04.990
And those higher order systems
create new sources of value

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:06.700
and new sources of worth.

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:09.550
So as electricity became a
utility, enabled television,

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:11.560
and television became
a new industry.

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:15.490
Of course, all this stuff
is uncertain on the left.

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:18.670
So you didn't know whether
the box in the corner showing

00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:21.290
pictures was going to
be the future success

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:24.490
or whether it was the
refrigeration blanket.

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:26.380
Personally I would have
gone for the blanket,

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:29.230
but it turned out to be the box.

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:31.780
So things evolve, new things
appear, they evolve, they

00:24:31.780 --> 00:24:32.950
enable new things to appear.

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:36.310
And so what you get is a line
of the present in a constant sea

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:39.816
of moving components.

00:24:39.816 --> 00:24:41.190
And then you learn
more patterns.

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:46.470
That we have inertia to change
because of past success.

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:48.930
So if you take somebody
like Blockbuster, Netflix,

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:52.270
who was first with a website?

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:52.930
Blockbuster.

00:24:52.930 --> 00:24:53.430
Right.

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:56.280
Who was first with
video ordering online?

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:57.330
Blockbuster, yay.

00:24:57.330 --> 00:25:02.800
Who was first with experimenting
with streaming video online?

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:03.990
Blockbuster, yay.

00:25:03.990 --> 00:25:05.275
Who went bankrupt?

00:25:05.275 --> 00:25:06.421
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:06.421 --> 00:25:06.920
Right.

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:09.280
So anybody who says, you
don't want to be uninnovative

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:13.350
like Blockbuster,
well, actually, they

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.100
pretty much out-innovated
most people.

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:20.370
Their problem-- do you know
what their problem was?

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.742
Late fees.

00:25:22.742 --> 00:25:23.450
Hence the stores.

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:26.570
Late fees, they were
addicted to late fees.

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:28.070
For those of you
who don't remember,

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:29.903
you would get a video,
it's a cassette thing

00:25:29.903 --> 00:25:32.600
which you'd get from the shop,
go home, forget to take it back

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:36.550
the next day, and get
charged a fortune.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:38.560
So now we have some
basic patterns,

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:41.530
we can anticipate,
communicate, and challenge.

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:44.440
So I took my map of
online photo service,

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:47.890
I knew the computing platform
was evolving to a utility.

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:50.380
I knew that we would have
inertia to the change.

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:53.930
I knew that this would enable
higher order systems to appear

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:56.620
and that some of them were
new sources of value or worth

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:59.600
and we would get a new
line of the future.

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:01.956
So there we were,
sitting in the boardroom.

00:26:01.956 --> 00:26:05.050
I had IT, I had business
development, finance,

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:06.160
operations, marketing.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:09.310
We're all sitting around
discussing, around a map,

00:26:09.310 --> 00:26:10.870
how our world is changing.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:14.260
A bit like, in the
military, a marine

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:17.620
needs to call in air support.

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:19.870
They can use a map to do so.

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:21.400
You don't phone up--

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:23.740
oh, sorry, or call in on
a radio and get somebody

00:26:23.740 --> 00:26:25.640
from air support, the Air Force.

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:29.230
And they turn around and say,
do you know how to fly a jet?

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:29.760
No.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:31.780
Well, we can't support you then.

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:34.249
But interestingly, we
do that in business.

00:26:34.249 --> 00:26:36.040
You may have noticed
everybody in IT thinks

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:37.420
everybody else should learn IT.

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:39.400
And everybody in finance
thinks everybody else

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:40.740
should learn finance.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.220
And so we didn't
have that problem.

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:44.220
That was nice.

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:47.250
And then we learned
about weak signals.

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:48.180
Weak signals.

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:50.660
A friend of mine used to run
a hedge fund, greed is good,

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:52.590
all that sort of stuff.

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:55.320
They wanted to use
social networks

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:59.010
to look at where their
companies were possibly thinking

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:00.750
about acquiring others.

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:03.420
And I explained, that's
not the way you do it

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:06.750
because CEOs don't tend to
like each other on Facebook

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:10.710
or whatever, especially not
prior to an acquisition.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:12.749
But what they do
like is private jets

00:27:12.749 --> 00:27:14.540
and the tailplane
numbers are public and so

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:15.460
are the flight plans.

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:17.251
So what you can do is
just map the movement

00:27:17.251 --> 00:27:19.770
of all the private jets,
looking for disturbances

00:27:19.770 --> 00:27:20.530
in the pattern.

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:22.690
This was 2008.

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:25.107
They're very good at
leaking information.

00:27:25.107 --> 00:27:26.190
Everything must be secret.

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:28.023
We jump into our jet
and we leak information

00:27:28.023 --> 00:27:29.610
because it's all public.

00:27:29.610 --> 00:27:33.534
Anyway, the point is,
you can use weak signals

00:27:33.534 --> 00:27:34.200
with this stuff.

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:36.450
So we knew compute would
evolve to a utility.

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:38.970
We knew people
would have inertia.

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:42.300
We knew that would enable
higher order systems to appear.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.360
And this sort of change is known
as a punctuated equilibrium.

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.270
It's a non-linear
form of change.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:50.280
Now this is part
of a cycle known

00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:52.280
as Peace, War, and Wonder.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:55.439
It goes at both a local
and a macroeconomic scale.

00:27:55.439 --> 00:27:55.980
What happens?

00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:57.690
You get big vendors
in this space,

00:27:57.690 --> 00:27:59.550
whatever it happens to be.

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:02.880
They all have inertia to
change because of past success.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:06.390
New entrants, unencumbered by
a preexisting business model,

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:10.200
move in, like a
bookseller for hardware.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:12.990
That causes an explosion
of higher order systems,

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:15.990
the wonder out of which
new industries form,

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:17.490
and of course, all
the people stuck

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:21.720
behind the inertia barriers,
they disappear off the cliff.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:26.790
And this occurs at both a
local and a macro scale.

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:29.970
At the macro, we call them
ages, so Age of Electricity

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:31.800
didn't start with
the Parthian battery.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:35.070
It started with
industrialization

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:38.640
of electricity preexisting
at Tesla and Westinghouse.

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.740
Same as the mechanical age--

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:41.310
Machine Age.

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:44.540
Didn't start with the
formation of nuts and bolts.

00:28:44.540 --> 00:28:46.100
That was long before.

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:48.780
It started with
industrialization, that shift

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:51.700
from product to more commodity.

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:55.960
The point about this is, this
is the trigger, the shift

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:58.420
from product to commodity.

00:28:58.420 --> 00:29:00.426
And that's based upon
that evolution curve,

00:29:00.426 --> 00:29:02.800
which comes from this, which
comes from those publication

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:03.950
types.

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:06.880
So you can actually use the
publication types and variants

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:11.910
within them as a weak signal for
when these changes will occur.

00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:15.040
And that's the last time I
did a weak signal analysis.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:18.460
It's 2014, so it's
three years old.

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:21.954
And what you've got is big data
is just entering the war, 2015,

00:29:21.954 --> 00:29:24.370
which basically means you've
got loads of big data product

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:26.328
vendors running around
saying we're the future.

00:29:26.328 --> 00:29:28.840
You've got about 10 or 15
years before they're all

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:30.110
disappearing.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:33.070
It's already moving
into utility space.

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:34.780
That's already happening.

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:36.730
Platform, we're in that space.

00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:40.565
Center as a service,
a bit further away.

00:29:40.565 --> 00:29:42.720
But anyway, that
comes from the data.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:44.580
And the point about
this is you know

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:47.590
that these points
of industrialization

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:51.130
are approaching, so you
can at least plan for it.

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:52.720
A quick recap.

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:55.000
I wanted to be over
here, not over here.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:56.650
I hadn't done an MBA.

00:29:56.650 --> 00:30:01.090
This is obviously the stuff they
teach people at MBA's, but I

00:30:01.090 --> 00:30:04.430
had to invent it because
I couldn't afford an MBA.

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:06.790
I started off with
the strategy cycle.

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:11.140
Understand, observe your
environment and landscape

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:12.884
climate, orientate
with doctrine,

00:30:12.884 --> 00:30:14.050
then you're into leadership.

00:30:14.050 --> 00:30:16.960
It's a cycle, it's
an iterative process.

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:21.275
Key for this is understanding
your landscape, your map.

00:30:21.275 --> 00:30:24.700
It has some basic
characteristics.

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:26.810
You start with user needs,
understand position,

00:30:26.810 --> 00:30:28.132
understand movement.

00:30:28.132 --> 00:30:29.590
Once you've got
that, you can start

00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:33.820
to learn climatic patterns,
like everything evolves,

00:30:33.820 --> 00:30:36.640
characteristics change,
no one size fits all,

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:39.610
efficiency enables innovation,
higher order systems

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:41.860
create new sources
of value and worth,

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:45.300
past success creates
inertia, economy has cycles,

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:48.310
and you get things like
punctuated equilibriums.

00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:50.620
And that enables you
to do amazing acts

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:53.950
of fairly obvious prediction.

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:55.690
This stuff is going
to a utility and it's

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:58.671
going to enable other
stuff to appear.

00:30:58.671 --> 00:30:59.170
OK.

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:06.380
Now I get into doctrine because
once you've learned the map

00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:08.300
and you start to learn
climatic patterns,

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:11.180
you start to find
universal approaches.

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:13.980
By universal, I mean
they're useful everywhere.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:17.600
So things like if you're
in a military campaign,

00:31:17.600 --> 00:31:19.940
you get things like flanking
movements, which depend

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:21.610
upon where your opponents are.

00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:24.410
But there's useful
things like soldiers

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:29.134
learning to shoot a
rifle before a battle.

00:31:29.134 --> 00:31:31.550
It's not much use going, oh,
we should flank the opponent.

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:33.258
You better all run
off and start learning

00:31:33.258 --> 00:31:35.840
how to shoot a rifle
for the first time.

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:38.940
There is universal stuff
that happens before.

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:41.060
So these are universally
applicable principles,

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:43.040
regardless of context.

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:45.020
So things like,
focus on user needs,

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:46.955
turns out, is
universally useful.

00:31:49.760 --> 00:31:51.560
Reduce duplication.

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:54.470
One of the beauties about
maps is, if you share maps--

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:57.920
like immigration, borders
police, UK government-- you

00:31:57.920 --> 00:32:00.350
share maps between
departments, you suddenly

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:04.340
start discovering we're building
the same things elsewhere.

00:32:04.340 --> 00:32:06.470
Now, I used to think
the government was

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:09.320
bad at duplication
and waste, then

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:11.750
I started looking at
the private sector.

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:13.560
It's amazing.

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:15.470
So I thought really
bad would be having

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:18.800
six examples of the same
system in an organization.

00:32:18.800 --> 00:32:20.670
No one would ever beat that.

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:24.860
Then I found a national
bank with 14 CRM systems.

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:27.380
European corporate,
22 rules engines.

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:31.460
I had 118 workflow systems
registering prisoners

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:32.170
into prisons.

00:32:32.170 --> 00:32:34.670
We had to do it 118 times.

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:38.300
Then I found a global technology
vendor, 170 Cloud projects all

00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:39.620
doing the same thing.

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:42.760
A global pharma company,
300 separate teams

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:44.510
all building enterprise
content management

00:32:44.510 --> 00:32:46.940
systems and five global
efforts, none of which

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:49.170
knew the others existed.

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:51.920
A global energy
company, 380 teams

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:55.520
building customized versions
of the same ERP system.

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:59.685
And then I found a bank with
1,000 risk management systems.

00:32:59.685 --> 00:33:02.060
And they were all sitting
there going, we can't innovate.

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:06.360
And you just go, I
think I know why.

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:10.280
Anyway, if we take something
like HS2, heavy engineering,

00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:11.840
high speed rail.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:13.430
We used to try and
organize things

00:33:13.430 --> 00:33:15.590
with box and wire diagrams.

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:18.440
This is actually a map of them
building the entire railway

00:33:18.440 --> 00:33:21.470
in a virtual world because
it is actually much more

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:24.170
sensible to dig up a virtual
world and go, whoops,

00:33:24.170 --> 00:33:26.540
we got it wrong, than
in the real world.

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:30.980
But you've got a map and now
what they're do is, we check.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:33.087
Have we got systems elsewhere?

00:33:33.087 --> 00:33:34.670
Are we using the
right sort of method?

00:33:34.670 --> 00:33:36.470
We're going to outsource
that stuff on the right,

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:38.210
build off-the-shelf
product-- oh, sorry.

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:40.470
Yeah, use off-the-shelf
products in the middle,

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:43.400
build agile in-house
techniques on the left.

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:45.960
This is 2011, 2012.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:49.280
Point is, we started using,
now, multiple methods.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:53.960
That's another form of doctrine,
using appropriate methods.

00:33:53.960 --> 00:33:56.630
In the past, we do things
like take out topographical--

00:33:56.630 --> 00:33:57.130
sorry.

00:33:57.130 --> 00:34:01.000
A box and wire, a
network topology diagram,

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:02.740
and go and ask things
like, should we

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:05.650
outsource this without a map?

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:07.210
And we'd make a decision.

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:10.210
And of course, if you map
that version, what you find

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:13.030
is that some bits are actually
suitable for mapping--

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:14.980
for outsourcing, I should say--

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:18.159
and some bits, on the left-hand
side, are novel and new.

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:20.199
So you wouldn't outsource them.

00:34:20.199 --> 00:34:24.159
If you do, you'll just incur
excessive change control costs

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:27.090
because they will
change all the time.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.750
So you don't take a whole
bunch of things like this,

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:32.960
get a big specification
to outsource,

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:36.500
because you will just end
up with huge overruns.

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:38.840
But that's what we'd
done in the past.

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:40.797
And then we'd have
fights with the vendors.

00:34:40.797 --> 00:34:43.130
And then somebody, some Muppet,
would come out with the,

00:34:43.130 --> 00:34:46.219
well, we need better
specification in the future.

00:34:46.219 --> 00:34:49.370
Well, that's never,
ever going to work.

00:34:49.370 --> 00:34:53.120
To give you an example of this,
I thought I'd do an experiment.

00:34:53.120 --> 00:34:56.750
Do you know what a world
perception server is?

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:57.250
No?

00:34:57.250 --> 00:34:58.480
OK.

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:01.240
It's part of a self-driving car.

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:05.650
And what I've done is, I've
translated the network topology

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:07.780
diagram all into Elvish.

00:35:07.780 --> 00:35:09.580
So I'm going to pretend
you're all finance.

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:13.720
And IT is all Elvish
to finance people.

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:17.080
So here is the network
diagram in Elvish.

00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:20.020
And so I'm going to say,
should we outsource or build

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:23.500
our own A, should we
outsource or build our own B?

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:25.750
What do you think?

00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:29.320
This is the sort of decision
processes that we go through.

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:31.390
Anybody?

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:32.010
OK.

00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:34.740
What I'm now going to do is I'm
going to convert it into a map.

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:38.100
Exactly the same diagram,
but it's now a map.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:42.220
Should we outsource
or build our own A?

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:43.130
OK.

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:43.720
Wow.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:45.702
What about B?

00:35:45.702 --> 00:35:46.589
Fantastic.

00:35:46.589 --> 00:35:48.130
And of course, now,
translate it back

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:50.470
into English, GPS, and
world perception server.

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:52.990
But the point is, we
make these decisions

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:55.450
about outsourcing
contracts not using--

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:58.060
well, using those
sort of box and wires.

00:35:58.060 --> 00:36:00.220
Anyway, you get to
the point of now

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:01.690
you're using
appropriate methods.

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:03.273
And then we get onto
things like FIST,

00:36:03.273 --> 00:36:05.110
fast, inexpensive,
simple, and tiny.

00:36:05.110 --> 00:36:08.530
US Air Force, Lieutenant
Colonel Dan Ward.

00:36:08.530 --> 00:36:11.920
It's an approach of breaking
down large complex systems

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:13.540
into small pieces.

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:16.380
It's used by Special
Operations Command.

00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:18.269
What you do is
you've got your map,

00:36:18.269 --> 00:36:19.560
you're using the right methods.

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:20.710
Now you think small.

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:23.080
You're building small teams.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:26.720
Amazon now calls that two-pizza,
Hire has its own cell base

00:36:26.720 --> 00:36:27.220
structure.

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:28.840
It's the same thing.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:34.350
And then what you discover is
that attitudes are different.

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:37.662
So engineering over
here is not the same

00:36:37.662 --> 00:36:39.620
as engineering over here,
and it's not the same

00:36:39.620 --> 00:36:40.828
as engineering in the middle.

00:36:40.828 --> 00:36:42.620
Same with finance and marketing.

00:36:42.620 --> 00:36:45.170
So you start building
structures which

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:48.190
cope with evolving attitudes.

00:36:48.190 --> 00:36:51.350
You have pioneers,
settlers, and town planners.

00:36:51.350 --> 00:36:52.940
And you create, from
that, structures

00:36:52.940 --> 00:36:55.340
which are constantly
evolving, cell based,

00:36:55.340 --> 00:36:56.940
considering attitude.

00:36:56.940 --> 00:36:58.830
We don't have time
to go through that.

00:36:58.830 --> 00:37:01.880
But GCHQ, that's our
intelligence services,

00:37:01.880 --> 00:37:05.120
very kindly open-source
Boiling Frogs,

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:08.450
which is a document which talks
about how you use structure

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:10.770
around constant change.

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:11.270
OK.

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:12.950
Quick recap.

00:37:12.950 --> 00:37:15.199
Strategy cycle.

00:37:15.199 --> 00:37:17.240
Purpose, landscap, climate,
doctrine, leadership.

00:37:17.240 --> 00:37:18.440
Very basic, very simple.

00:37:18.440 --> 00:37:19.066
It's iterative.

00:37:19.066 --> 00:37:20.440
It's all about
the why of purpose

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:21.515
and the why of movement.

00:37:21.515 --> 00:37:24.110
So observe your environment,
then orientate and decide

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:25.430
in that.

00:37:25.430 --> 00:37:27.890
To start with, you
need to have a map.

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:30.470
A map has a number of
basic characteristics,

00:37:30.470 --> 00:37:33.152
context-specific, position,
anchor, and movement.

00:37:33.152 --> 00:37:34.610
Once you've got
that, you can start

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:37.370
to learn climatic patterns,
things like everything

00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:42.140
evolves, characteristics
change, no one size fits all,

00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:44.990
efficiency enables innovation,
higher order systems

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:46.880
create new sources of worth.

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:49.450
That we have inertia
caused by past success,

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:51.890
the economy has cycles,
there are things

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:53.810
called punctuated equilibriums.

00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:55.490
In fact, what you discover--

00:37:55.490 --> 00:37:57.080
prepare yourself for horror--

00:37:57.080 --> 00:38:00.570
there are 27 different
common economic patterns.

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:02.720
And so you get to-- it's
like rules of the game.

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:05.120
You get to learn these and
then you can more effectively

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:06.700
anticipate.

00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:09.410
And you can just
take your map, you

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:11.330
can describe the likely
points of change,

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:15.110
you can start using weak
signals to refine on that.

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:17.330
Then you get into doctrine.

00:38:17.330 --> 00:38:19.550
We now have an idea of what's
in our landscape, where

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:20.050
it's going.

00:38:20.050 --> 00:38:22.310
So how do we organize ourselves?

00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:24.860
You think about, well, I've
got to focus on user needs.

00:38:24.860 --> 00:38:27.389
I'm going to reduce
duplication using multiple maps

00:38:27.389 --> 00:38:28.430
between different groups.

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:30.330
Use appropriate
methods, think small,

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:31.850
think aptitude and attitude.

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:34.580
Design for constant evolution.

00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:35.790
Prepare yourself for shock.

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:39.080
There's about 40 common
forms of universal doctrine.

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:39.980
Really useful stuff.

00:38:39.980 --> 00:38:43.340
Actually, this is quite
useful looking at competitors

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:46.192
because you get an idea
of just how bad they are.

00:38:46.192 --> 00:38:49.561
To which people then
go, ah, this is complex.

00:38:49.561 --> 00:38:50.060
And it is.

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:52.590
That's why we like stories.

00:38:52.590 --> 00:38:55.460
That's why we like
secrets of success.

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:58.490
That's why we like
magic frameworks.

00:38:58.490 --> 00:39:01.630
It is complex, I'm afraid.

00:39:01.630 --> 00:39:04.380
Then we get into
the leadership bit,

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:07.380
which is all about context
specific forms of gameplay.

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:09.150
So you have your map.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:10.740
You can anticipate
where things are

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:13.380
going to go because you've
got those climatic patterns.

00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:16.620
Not everything is perfect, but
you can do a reasonable job.

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:19.097
And now you're going,
where do I attack?

00:39:19.097 --> 00:39:20.430
And this is the why of movement.

00:39:20.430 --> 00:39:21.607
Why here over there?

00:39:21.607 --> 00:39:23.190
Do I want to build
the first platform,

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:25.140
the first compute environment?

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:28.280
Do I want to modify the
online photo service?

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:29.820
You also learn
where not to attack.

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:33.900
Where to leave it, all the stuff
stuck behind inertia barriers.

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:38.130
And then you start to learn you
can manipulate the environment.

00:39:38.130 --> 00:39:40.290
So you can use open
approaches to accelerate,

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:44.115
you can slow things down with
fear, uncertainty, and doubt.

00:39:44.115 --> 00:39:45.000
Pattern plays.

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:47.040
There's about four or
five different forms

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:48.310
of ecosystem models.

00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:52.350
There's all sorts of constraints
that you can manipulate.

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:53.490
Horror.

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:56.880
There's about 68 different ways
of manipulating the market.

00:39:56.880 --> 00:39:57.990
It's great fun.

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:01.370
And so you come up with
a context specific play.

00:40:01.370 --> 00:40:04.340
And that's what
we did in Fotango.

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:06.399
2005, we used the map.

00:40:06.399 --> 00:40:08.940
We decided to build the world's
first serverless environment.

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:11.610
Platform as a service, or it was
called framework as a service,

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:13.110
originally.

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:14.370
JavaScript front and back end.

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:15.703
You build an entire application.

00:40:15.703 --> 00:40:17.070
It had functional billing.

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:19.590
It was all based on JavaScript
operations, network,

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:20.730
and storage.

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:23.450
We anticipated
somebody would play

00:40:23.450 --> 00:40:25.040
a utility compute environment.

00:40:25.040 --> 00:40:26.915
I actually thought it
was going to be Google.

00:40:26.915 --> 00:40:29.400
Didn't realize it was going to
be Amazon the following year

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:30.870
with EC2.

00:40:30.870 --> 00:40:32.695
We had a whole open
play around it.

00:40:32.695 --> 00:40:35.070
And that's what we went and
did because the next thing is

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:35.570
you act.

00:40:35.570 --> 00:40:38.850
So we built and launched Zimki
and it grew like hot cakes.

00:40:38.850 --> 00:40:41.760
And we were like, it's great.

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:45.310
People building entire
new applications in a day.

00:40:45.310 --> 00:40:46.717
Fabulous.

00:40:46.717 --> 00:40:48.550
And then what happened
is the parent company

00:40:48.550 --> 00:40:52.704
got some consultants in and
they basically said, oh,

00:40:52.704 --> 00:40:54.370
there are three things
that we're doing.

00:40:54.370 --> 00:40:56.260
3D printing, mobile
phones as cameras,

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:58.990
and this utility
computing thing.

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:00.920
That's not the future.

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:03.100
The future is 3D TV.

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:06.400
So shut it all down and
spend a billion on 3D TV.

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:10.320
By the way, who owns a 3D TV?

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:13.330
Who uses mobile
phone as a camera?

00:41:13.330 --> 00:41:16.010
Who uses Cloud?

00:41:16.010 --> 00:41:17.500
3D printers?

00:41:17.500 --> 00:41:20.050
I don't like American
strategy consultancy firms.

00:41:20.050 --> 00:41:22.230
Anyway.

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:23.050
Anyway, anyway.

00:41:23.050 --> 00:41:24.670
So I went to a
company called Ubuntu.

00:41:24.670 --> 00:41:25.847
Heard of Ubuntu?

00:41:25.847 --> 00:41:27.310
Yay.

00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:29.190
So in 2008--

00:41:29.190 --> 00:41:30.220
Mark's a friend of mine.

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:33.430
We mapped out the environment,
determined where to attack.

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:35.870
I also mapped out Red
Hat and Microsoft,

00:41:35.870 --> 00:41:38.777
and specifically, with Red Hat,
looked for points of inertia

00:41:38.777 --> 00:41:40.610
and how we could play
the game against them,

00:41:40.610 --> 00:41:42.460
et cetera, et cetera.

00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:44.260
Particular things like
where their salesmen

00:41:44.260 --> 00:41:47.230
had inertia to change and
how we could exploit that.

00:41:47.230 --> 00:41:50.130
So we exploited that, and
basically half a million--

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:53.000
18 months later, we went from
2% operating system market

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:54.140
to 70% of Cloud.

00:41:54.140 --> 00:41:55.000
Wee.

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:56.210
That wasn't too bad.

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:58.660
And then I went to
government, wrote something

00:41:58.660 --> 00:42:00.010
called the "Better for Less."

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:00.580
Well, sorry.

00:42:00.580 --> 00:42:02.087
I worked for a
research organization

00:42:02.087 --> 00:42:04.420
called the Leading Edge Forum,
where I did a lot of work

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:06.700
with Liam Maxwell and
with the government.

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:10.240
And this was for Francis
Moore before the coalition

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:11.982
got into power.

00:42:11.982 --> 00:42:14.440
So that led to lots of things
like spin control and changes

00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:15.730
in the UK government.

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:17.190
So a quick summary.

00:42:17.190 --> 00:42:19.390
Strategy's actually
cycle, not a linear path.

00:42:19.390 --> 00:42:21.220
It's an iterative process.

00:42:21.220 --> 00:42:23.910
It's like chess.

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:26.170
It's a game you have to
learn, it takes time.

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:28.670
It helps to
understand the board.

00:42:28.670 --> 00:42:30.550
One of the key things
is, acting movement

00:42:30.550 --> 00:42:32.420
is the mechanism of learning.

00:42:32.420 --> 00:42:36.040
Really important to understand
the landscape, as well,

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:37.520
to observe it.

00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:41.920
So it's all about small steps,
understanding the environment,

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:42.910
being adaptive.

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:44.950
Which is basically
what crossing the river

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:49.220
by feeling the stones, Deng
Xiaoping, is all about.

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:51.544
Now if you're all going,
gah, that mapping, I

00:42:51.544 --> 00:42:53.210
don't understand any
of it, don't worry.

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:54.760
It's all Creative Commons.

00:42:54.760 --> 00:42:58.305
You'll find
wardleymaps.com has--

00:42:58.305 --> 00:42:59.800
I've got a Medium account.

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:01.850
You can read all
about it on there.

00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:05.080
And there's a group called Map
Camp setting up to teach people

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:06.430
how to map, as well.

00:43:06.430 --> 00:43:08.170
And that's me.

00:43:08.170 --> 00:43:09.060
Thank you.

00:43:09.060 --> 00:43:13.060
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:16.190 --> 00:43:16.690
OK.

00:43:16.690 --> 00:43:17.640
Future things to think about.

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:19.098
I had to charge
through all of that

00:43:19.098 --> 00:43:22.660
because I just wanted
to get to this point.

00:43:22.660 --> 00:43:24.659
I assume-- how many of
you-- actually, I should

00:43:24.659 --> 00:43:25.700
have asked that question.

00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:27.717
How many of you map?

00:43:27.717 --> 00:43:28.800
There's only a few of you.

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:29.550
Hey!

00:43:29.550 --> 00:43:31.042
Well, there's some
of you, anyway.

00:43:31.042 --> 00:43:33.000
There's three other topics
I can quickly cover.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:37.210
A serverless ecosystem and MAGA.

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:38.740
That's actually a
spelling mistake.

00:43:38.740 --> 00:43:41.610
It should be MAGBA, Make
America Great Britain Again.

00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:43.180
Anyway, sorry.

00:43:46.850 --> 00:43:47.760
Or quick questions.

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:49.661
Do you want me to
go through these?

00:43:49.661 --> 00:43:50.160
Yes?

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:50.660
OK.

00:43:50.660 --> 00:43:52.350
So serverless and co-evolution.

00:43:52.350 --> 00:43:53.970
Right.

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:56.820
So our platform, really
interesting point of war again.

00:43:56.820 --> 00:43:58.130
2014, we're in it.

00:43:58.130 --> 00:44:00.929
It's shifting into that
utility space, code execution

00:44:00.929 --> 00:44:01.470
environments.

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:02.340
Right.

00:44:02.340 --> 00:44:05.410
Let's wind back a little bit,
talk about infrastructure.

00:44:05.410 --> 00:44:06.000
So compute.

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:10.410
When compute was a product,
we built applications

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:14.490
which used emerging
architectural practices based

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:16.410
upon compute as a product.

00:44:16.410 --> 00:44:19.290
And these were all based
upon the idea of high MTTR.

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:21.780
So I had a server and
if my server went bang,

00:44:21.780 --> 00:44:23.670
it would take me weeks
to get a new one.

00:44:23.670 --> 00:44:26.070
So I had architectural
practices like scale up,

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:32.720
capacity planning, n+1,
disaster recovery test.

00:44:32.720 --> 00:44:35.700
And do you all remember those?

00:44:35.700 --> 00:44:36.340
Yes?

00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:36.840
Yes.

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:37.340
Great.

00:44:37.340 --> 00:44:37.890
Super.

00:44:37.890 --> 00:44:42.810
And so they evolved and became
best practice applications

00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:44.850
built with best architectural
practice for use

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:46.170
as a server as a product.

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:48.120
And we would mock
people who didn't

00:44:48.120 --> 00:44:51.030
do capacity planning and
all that sort of, oh, you

00:44:51.030 --> 00:44:53.377
silly person, et cetera.

00:44:53.377 --> 00:44:55.710
And then, of course, what
happened is, computer evolved,

00:44:55.710 --> 00:44:57.720
became more of a utility.

00:44:57.720 --> 00:44:59.490
And so you've got a
new set of emerging

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:03.600
architectural practices based
upon the characteristics which

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:05.070
are low MTTR.

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:07.290
So what happens is
your server goes bang,

00:45:07.290 --> 00:45:09.510
takes you seconds to recover.

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:12.210
So you've now got things like
distributed systems designed

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:14.310
for failure chaos engines.

00:45:14.310 --> 00:45:16.950
And that evolved and it's
become good practice.

00:45:16.950 --> 00:45:19.572
And that's DevOps
and that's legacy.

00:45:19.572 --> 00:45:21.030
So when we talk
about legacy, we're

00:45:21.030 --> 00:45:23.580
talking about applications
built with best

00:45:23.580 --> 00:45:25.900
architectural practice
for product world.

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:27.183
And that is legacy.

00:45:27.183 --> 00:45:30.600
And DevOps is applications,
good architectural practice

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:32.060
for utility world.

00:45:32.060 --> 00:45:33.060
OK?

00:45:33.060 --> 00:45:35.640
And of course, what happens
is, people go, make my legacy

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:39.571
cloudy, i.e., they want to have
all the benefits of efficiency,

00:45:39.571 --> 00:45:41.070
agility, and all
that sort of stuff.

00:45:41.070 --> 00:45:42.870
So I want to take
that stuff, but I

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:44.982
don't want to re-architect.

00:45:44.982 --> 00:45:46.440
And so somebody
sticks their legacy

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:47.440
on something like Amazon.

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:49.410
Amazon has an outage and
they run around going,

00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:52.390
the end of cloud is nigh.

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:55.050
To which you go, shouldn't our
architecture evolve, as well?

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:56.400
And they go, burn him.

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:57.200
Evil.

00:45:57.200 --> 00:45:59.550
And get her, get her.

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:01.720
A heretic, witch, or
whatever they say.

00:46:01.720 --> 00:46:02.220
Right.

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:06.090
So the point is, it's
those people, their best

00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:09.067
architectural practice, they
have inertia to this change

00:46:09.067 --> 00:46:10.650
and I don't like
this, it's uncertain,

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:12.180
et cetera, et cetera.

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:15.830
Anyway, if you move up
the stack into platform,

00:46:15.830 --> 00:46:19.925
we've got this shift going
on from very much a product

00:46:19.925 --> 00:46:20.425
space--

00:46:20.425 --> 00:46:23.700
I've got a LAMP stack or
whatever it happens to be--

00:46:23.700 --> 00:46:26.040
much more to a utility
execution environment.

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:28.920
I just write code, et cetera,
I deal with these functions.

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:31.260
And you'll get a
co-evolution of practice.

00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:34.350
So a whole set of new
financial development practices

00:46:34.350 --> 00:46:37.170
will come out, particularly
things like functional billing.

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:40.530
It changes the way you operate,
how you invest in things,

00:46:40.530 --> 00:46:42.900
refactoring all
this sort of stuff.

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:44.280
And that'll spread.

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:46.680
And so you should be
investing in those two places.

00:46:46.680 --> 00:46:48.660
Of course, you're
going to get resistance

00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:51.050
from people, best
architectural practice for how

00:46:51.050 --> 00:46:54.422
you build LAMP stack of
whatever, legacy environments.

00:46:54.422 --> 00:46:56.130
And you're always
going to get resistance

00:46:56.130 --> 00:46:58.630
from the DevOps people because
they're now below the line.

00:46:58.630 --> 00:46:59.910
So they don't matter as much.

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:02.690
They're going to
become less visible.

00:47:02.690 --> 00:47:05.320
And they're not
going to like that.

00:47:05.320 --> 00:47:07.310
So they're all going
to give you inertia.

00:47:07.310 --> 00:47:07.810
OK.

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:10.365
So you'll go out and say, how
about no Ops, and they're like,

00:47:10.365 --> 00:47:12.980
burn you, heretic, and
all the rest of it.

00:47:12.980 --> 00:47:14.130
It's tough.

00:47:14.130 --> 00:47:16.860
That's the way it goes.

00:47:16.860 --> 00:47:18.630
Ecosystem.

00:47:18.630 --> 00:47:22.950
If you look at this game, if you
look at somebody like Lambda,

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:26.100
of course there's [INAUDIBLE]
functions and Google Cloud

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:27.059
functions, as well.

00:47:27.059 --> 00:47:28.350
Make some noise for it, please.

00:47:31.470 --> 00:47:33.350
If you look at
somebody like Lambda,

00:47:33.350 --> 00:47:36.010
Amazon is very good at
a particular eco-- well,

00:47:36.010 --> 00:47:38.370
it's very good at
ecosystems play.

00:47:38.370 --> 00:47:40.560
At this point, I
have to be careful.

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:43.230
Because we cannot say
how they play the game,

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:47.920
but I'll show you an example
of how you might play the game.

00:47:47.920 --> 00:47:50.630
So it's a game play called
ILC, innovate, leverage,

00:47:50.630 --> 00:47:51.390
commoditize.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:53.640
It's a decade old.

00:47:53.640 --> 00:47:55.140
It's 11 years old, this one.

00:47:55.140 --> 00:47:57.210
I know, because I wrote
about it in Zimki.

00:47:57.210 --> 00:47:58.360
It's very simple.

00:47:58.360 --> 00:48:00.580
You take an activity,
you commoditize it

00:48:00.580 --> 00:48:03.720
to a component service,
provide it as a utility.

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:07.560
Everybody builds new things on
top, componentization effects.

00:48:07.560 --> 00:48:10.200
They act as your free research
and development department.

00:48:10.200 --> 00:48:11.810
Thank you very much.

00:48:11.810 --> 00:48:15.630
[? My ?] metadata to identify
new components, which you

00:48:15.630 --> 00:48:17.790
provide as utility services.

00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:21.006
The group over here grumble, oh,
he's eating my business model.

00:48:21.006 --> 00:48:23.970
And that enables new higher
order systems to appear

00:48:23.970 --> 00:48:25.800
and everybody thinks
you're brilliant.

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:30.190
And so what you've got is
others innovating for you.

00:48:30.190 --> 00:48:31.990
That's your entire ecosystem.

00:48:31.990 --> 00:48:35.460
And you're mining metadata
to find future patterns

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:39.930
and you've got an ever-growing
platform of commodity services,

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:41.940
which everybody is building on.

00:48:41.940 --> 00:48:44.160
You normally show
it in this form.

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:46.170
You take something
from the ecosystem,

00:48:46.170 --> 00:48:48.690
turn it into a utility
service, expose in the API,

00:48:48.690 --> 00:48:50.400
everybody builds on top.

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:52.350
Use that to identify
new patterns

00:48:52.350 --> 00:48:56.010
by leveraging the data,
metadata, consumption data, not

00:48:56.010 --> 00:48:57.570
their actual data.

00:48:57.570 --> 00:48:59.790
And then you use it
to build new services.

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:01.480
And the net result,
it's very simple.

00:49:01.480 --> 00:49:03.450
Again, it's a very old model.

00:49:03.450 --> 00:49:05.640
Your apparent rate of
innovation, customer focus,

00:49:05.640 --> 00:49:08.280
and efficiency now all
simultaneously increase

00:49:08.280 --> 00:49:10.750
with the size of the ecosystem.

00:49:10.750 --> 00:49:13.620
So do you know a
company which seems

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:16.350
to be getting more
innovative, more efficient,

00:49:16.350 --> 00:49:18.900
more customer
focused every year,

00:49:18.900 --> 00:49:23.910
which other people say, they've
eaten my business model over?

00:49:23.910 --> 00:49:27.780
Anybody got any ideas
who that might be?

00:49:27.780 --> 00:49:28.540
Right.

00:49:28.540 --> 00:49:32.490
So if you are that
company and you're

00:49:32.490 --> 00:49:37.290
playing that utility game,
what's going to happen is-- let

00:49:37.290 --> 00:49:40.214
us suppose they are
providing a code execution

00:49:40.214 --> 00:49:42.630
environment with all those
benefits of functional billing.

00:49:42.630 --> 00:49:44.004
Then certainly
soon, you're going

00:49:44.004 --> 00:49:46.140
to have a marketplace
with functions

00:49:46.140 --> 00:49:48.990
you can buy from others, a
CPAN-like repository where

00:49:48.990 --> 00:49:50.910
you can get code
functions from others,

00:49:50.910 --> 00:49:53.100
and suddenly, you discover
the level of duplications

00:49:53.100 --> 00:49:56.970
in systems is
nothing in comparison

00:49:56.970 --> 00:49:59.790
to the level of
duplications within systems,

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:00.832
particularly within code.

00:50:00.832 --> 00:50:03.081
And you'll get into a world
where, suddenly, everybody

00:50:03.081 --> 00:50:05.220
is building entire
trading applications

00:50:05.220 --> 00:50:06.590
in a day and a half.

00:50:06.590 --> 00:50:09.450
I know, I built one in two
days, but that was 2006.

00:50:09.450 --> 00:50:12.750
So that's the power
of ecosystems.

00:50:12.750 --> 00:50:13.800
So really watch out.

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:17.610
Be careful if you discover that
somebody is playing a utility

00:50:17.610 --> 00:50:20.130
code execution game against
you with functional billing

00:50:20.130 --> 00:50:22.110
and is very good at ecosystems.

00:50:22.110 --> 00:50:26.010
Because that's a very dangerous
competitor and somebody

00:50:26.010 --> 00:50:28.140
you have to react to.

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:28.640
Right.

00:50:28.640 --> 00:50:29.280
MAGBA.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:32.154
Do you want to hear about MAGBA?

00:50:32.154 --> 00:50:33.570
We've only got a
few minutes left.

00:50:33.570 --> 00:50:33.750
Right.

00:50:33.750 --> 00:50:34.170
MAGBA.

00:50:34.170 --> 00:50:34.410
Right.

00:50:34.410 --> 00:50:35.118
Compared to what?

00:50:35.118 --> 00:50:39.120
Make America great
again compared to what?

00:50:39.120 --> 00:50:39.840
OK.

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:41.610
Self-driving cars,
because I like

00:50:41.610 --> 00:50:43.830
elves and self-driving cars.

00:50:43.830 --> 00:50:49.380
I did a piece of work years
ago on the automotive industry.

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:52.530
You start with consumers
want to go from A to B.

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:53.860
They want comfort, affordable.

00:50:53.860 --> 00:50:55.470
You've got a pipeline of cars.

00:50:55.470 --> 00:50:57.120
They also want
status because they

00:50:57.120 --> 00:50:58.717
want to look good in their car.

00:50:58.717 --> 00:51:00.300
And underneath that,
you've got things

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:04.350
like entertainment systems,
IS screens, location

00:51:04.350 --> 00:51:08.460
based services, IoT, power
supplies, assembly, sensors,

00:51:08.460 --> 00:51:10.564
a whole bunch of things.

00:51:10.564 --> 00:51:11.980
And what's
interesting, of course,

00:51:11.980 --> 00:51:16.430
is that if you roll it
forward, that's 2015--

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:20.724
hm, 2014-- but you roll
it forward to 2025,

00:51:20.724 --> 00:51:22.140
you find it's a
lot more commodity

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:23.264
and all that sort of stuff.

00:51:23.264 --> 00:51:24.362
Very interesting.

00:51:24.362 --> 00:51:25.320
And you find new links.

00:51:25.320 --> 00:51:26.820
And what it basically
means is we're

00:51:26.820 --> 00:51:29.220
heading into a world
which is all much more

00:51:29.220 --> 00:51:33.180
self-driving cars, immersive,
you won't own a car.

00:51:33.180 --> 00:51:34.980
You'll have a
digital subscription.

00:51:34.980 --> 00:51:36.390
You might have
gold, I might have

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:39.150
silver, which means when I'm
puttering along in my car,

00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:41.190
it'll get out of the
way as you zoom past me

00:51:41.190 --> 00:51:43.000
and all that sort of stuff.

00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:45.060
But it's really exciting.

00:51:45.060 --> 00:51:45.960
Not really.

00:51:45.960 --> 00:51:47.340
It's all fairly obvious.

00:51:47.340 --> 00:51:49.131
From a government point
of view, of course,

00:51:49.131 --> 00:51:50.910
it impacts things
like driving licenses

00:51:50.910 --> 00:51:54.160
and and you have to think
about that sort of stuff.

00:51:54.160 --> 00:51:56.770
But what's interesting
is now, if we start

00:51:56.770 --> 00:51:58.690
looking at the points of war--

00:51:58.690 --> 00:52:01.600
these points of heavy
industrialization--

00:52:01.600 --> 00:52:04.090
and then we start looking
at which governments

00:52:04.090 --> 00:52:07.640
are good at investing,
as encouraging industries

00:52:07.640 --> 00:52:09.940
prior to those
points of war, you

00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:11.530
find China's brilliant
at this stuff.

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:14.320
It's gotten really, really good.

00:52:14.320 --> 00:52:16.600
So we all run around going,
the future's Uber, Google,

00:52:16.600 --> 00:52:18.280
whatever, within self-driving.

00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:19.200
Well, it may be.

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:24.040
But actually, more like
[? DD, ?] et cetera.

00:52:24.040 --> 00:52:27.220
So the problem with the whole
"make America great again,"

00:52:27.220 --> 00:52:29.440
it's not really
your choice alone.

00:52:29.440 --> 00:52:31.780
You are in competition
with other nations.

00:52:31.780 --> 00:52:34.380
And if we start looking at the
different value chains and game

00:52:34.380 --> 00:52:40.300
play that's going on, there's
some real skilled game play.

00:52:40.300 --> 00:52:42.410
You start going, well
actually, there's

00:52:42.410 --> 00:52:47.070
some really fierce and capable
and skilled competition.

00:52:47.070 --> 00:52:50.770
There's some truly
amazing Chinese companies.

00:52:50.770 --> 00:52:54.650
But anyway, people run
around with it and say,

00:52:54.650 --> 00:52:55.690
we've got to do this.

00:52:55.690 --> 00:52:59.130
And so there's huge obstacles.

00:52:59.130 --> 00:53:03.550
And I've been trying to find
what the biggest obstacle is.

00:53:03.550 --> 00:53:09.610
And I think it's economic
thought, bizarrely enough.

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:14.260
I took about 160 odd
economists and various people

00:53:14.260 --> 00:53:18.670
in the economic field and asked
them a bunch of questions.

00:53:18.670 --> 00:53:23.080
So US-centric people,
US-centric economists,

00:53:23.080 --> 00:53:25.650
I asked them, on a scale of
centrally planned to laissez

00:53:25.650 --> 00:53:28.690
faire, to place a
bunch of economists.

00:53:28.690 --> 00:53:31.940
They had Marx, Keynes,
Smith, Hayek, Friedman.

00:53:31.940 --> 00:53:33.040
OK?

00:53:33.040 --> 00:53:36.610
And they were like, this is the
less effective communist model.

00:53:36.610 --> 00:53:39.220
This is the more effective
capitalist model.

00:53:39.220 --> 00:53:42.640
And that's how they thought the
US operates, very much Smith,

00:53:42.640 --> 00:53:44.020
Hayek, Friedman.

00:53:44.020 --> 00:53:47.090
And that's very much
how China operates.

00:53:47.090 --> 00:53:49.580
That's interesting
because then I went,

00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:51.880
and I kept within
Western philosophy,

00:53:51.880 --> 00:53:53.830
and asked a bunch of
European economists

00:53:53.830 --> 00:53:55.390
exactly the same question.

00:53:55.390 --> 00:53:57.490
What we got is Marx here,
Keynes, Smith, and Hayek

00:53:57.490 --> 00:54:01.340
in the middle, Friedman
right on the right.

00:54:01.340 --> 00:54:03.340
So they are very
much-- this is more

00:54:03.340 --> 00:54:06.260
of a social
capitalist type model.

00:54:06.260 --> 00:54:07.480
And that's where the US is.

00:54:07.480 --> 00:54:11.280
And this is where China
seems to be operating.

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:14.280
And so you're talking
Europe and the impression

00:54:14.280 --> 00:54:16.470
is that China is actually
operating as the world's

00:54:16.470 --> 00:54:18.950
largest venture capital firm.

00:54:18.950 --> 00:54:21.940
But of course, if
you're talking to people

00:54:21.940 --> 00:54:26.860
in this sort of frame, that
doesn't make any sense to them.

00:54:26.860 --> 00:54:31.470
So I think part of
the problem is, A,

00:54:31.470 --> 00:54:34.600
it's a question of
whether you understand

00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:37.060
the actual landscapes
that you're competing in.

00:54:37.060 --> 00:54:39.010
What patterns are
actually played?

00:54:39.010 --> 00:54:42.910
But it's also the economic
processes of thought.

00:54:42.910 --> 00:54:46.480
It's really difficult. You
sit there with US economists

00:54:46.480 --> 00:54:48.250
and say, oh, no, it's a VC firm.

00:54:48.250 --> 00:54:50.200
They operate like the
world's largest VC

00:54:50.200 --> 00:54:52.080
firm, incredibly skilled.

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:55.830
And it's like, people just
have difficulty getting that.

00:54:55.830 --> 00:54:59.036
Anyway, it's not
a zero sum game.

00:54:59.036 --> 00:55:02.137
The one piece of advice
I actually would have is

00:55:02.137 --> 00:55:03.970
there's an awful lot
of learning from China.

00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:06.160
There are some really
skilled companies now

00:55:06.160 --> 00:55:09.400
who have extended beyond many
of the management practices

00:55:09.400 --> 00:55:11.410
we have in the West.

00:55:11.410 --> 00:55:13.067
Anyway, that was it.

00:55:13.067 --> 00:55:13.900
Thank you very much.

00:55:13.900 --> 00:55:20.200
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:20.200 --> 00:55:22.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

